builds/codex-git-admin/slice.out:508:**What the push gate accepts.** A changed file is covered when its exact state at the pushed tip (content and mode, or deleted) was seen by a complete close or a clean fix check (one with no finding that needs a status), logged in this repository at a commit the tip descends from. A review on another branch or in another clone covers nothing. Content already on the default branch needs no coverage: the gate trusts the commit that `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` names (a symbolic ref to an existing `refs/remotes/origin/<branch>`, read locally, never set by the gate, never from `REVIEW_BASE` or config `base`), and only for a push to `origin`; any other remote, or a missing, detached or dangling origin/HEAD, keeps the full rule. A file whose diff the 120k cap cut is not covered, and a file changed on both sides of a merge needs a review that saw the merge. `builds/` files need no coverage. The tip also needs one complete close at it (or followed only by `builds/` commits), so the tests ran there. A refusal lists the uncovered files: review just those with `REVIEW_PATHS` and close that slice at the tip. **Update a reviewed branch by merging the default branch into it, never by rebasing** (a rebase needs coverage again). After a clean merge, re-close the existing review at the merge tip: the tests run there and no model is called. A file changed on both sides of the merge, conflict or not, and any file edited inside the merge commit itself, gets a slice review at the tip (`REVIEW_PATHS` = the files the refusal names) closed there; the re-close then refuses (with a fix check) or goes partial (without one) naming those same files, which is expected, and the slice's close is the one the push needs. Generated dependency lockfiles (package-lock.json, yarn.lock, pnpm-lock.yaml, Cargo.lock, go.sum and the others the gate lists, by exact file name in any folder) are exempt by default; the refusal and `--summary` name every exempt file as not reviewed, so the PR shows them. The operator can list more files in `coverageExempt` (globs, like `riskPaths`) or turn the defaults off with `"coverageExemptDefaults": false`: in the home config (`coverageExempt` also through `REVIEW_COVERAGE_EXEMPT`) or an untracked repo config, never a tracked one. A tracked repo config never drops these home-config values either: the home file's `coverageExempt`, `coverageExemptDefaults`, `grokMcpAllow`, `verifyCmd`, `classifier` and `base` stay in force over it. Agents don't add to the list on their own. A refusal for any other file whose diff alone is over the cap goes in the PR as a blocker for the operator, never as a shell command for them to paste.
builds/codex-git-admin/slice.out:617:./packs/review-gate/tests/review.config-trust.test.mjs
packs/review-gate/skills/codex/security-check/SKILL.md:8:Look at the committed diff for **security holes only**: trust boundaries crossed without a check, untrusted input reaching a shell, SQL, file paths, eval or HTML, authentication and authorization gaps, secrets in code or output, path traversal, unsafe deserialization, SSRF, and changes to dependencies, install scripts or CI. Correctness, style and performance are out of scope.
builds/codex-git-admin/brief.md:3:Finding (verbatim from the fix check): the worktree's own Git administration directory (`<common>/worktrees/<name>/`) is granted whole to a Codex worker (lib/agent.js ~line 475, `--add-dir "$gitdir"`). It holds the `commondir` file that tells Git which common directory (and so which `config` and `hooks/`) that worktree uses. A sandboxed Codex worker could rewrite `commondir` (or write `config.worktree`) to point at a directory it controls with its own `config` (core.fsmonitor, core.sshCommand, core.hooksPath, reviewgate.enabled) and objects/refs linked to the real ones. The orchestrator's next git command in that worktree, including the push, then runs that config outside the sandbox.
builds/codex-git-admin/brief.md:5:Goal: a Codex worker in a linked worktree can still `git add` + `git commit` (the builder path), and cannot change which config/hooks git uses there: `commondir`, `gitdir`, `config.worktree` (and anything else in the admin dir that redirects config, hooks or objects) stay unwritable.
builds/codex-git-admin/brief.md:9:- Whether Codex's sandbox can express "this dir writable except these files" (writable_roots + read-only subpaths in its config/`-c sandbox_workspace_write…`, or --add-dir on files). Read the installed Codex's own help/config docs; don't guess.
builds/codex-git-admin/brief.md:10:- Options, in order of preference: (a) Codex config that keeps commondir/gitdir/config.worktree read-only inside the granted dir; (b) granting only the subpaths a commit needs if Codex accepts file-level grants; (c) no admin-dir grant: the worker commits through some other safe path. Say which works and why.
