builds/codex-git-admin/fix-2.md:11:The fixed file allowlist breaks git add when core.splitIndex=true and Git needs to create or refresh the shared index. Git stores sharedindex.<hash> files inside the worktree administration directory; none receives a grant. This supported Git configuration previously worked with the whole-directory grant, but now staging can fail before the intended commit.
builds/codex-git-admin/fix-2.md:13:trigger: A linked-worktree Codex worker stages changes with core.splitIndex=true that exceed splitIndex.maxPercentChange, requiring a new shared index.
builds/codex-git-admin/fix-2.md:15:suggestion: Handle split-index repositories safely, or force ordinary index mode for worker Git commands without changing persistent configuration. Cover staging and committing with core.splitIndex enabled.
builds/codex-git-admin/fix-2.md:18:The sandbox pins that keep temp directories and inherited writable roots out of a Codex worker's write set are emitted only inside the validated linked-worktree branch (after the backlink check passes). A Codex worker whose cwd is a normal checkout, a subdirectory of a linked worktree, or a worktree whose gitdir/backlink validation fails gets no pins, so Codex's default writable roots ($TMPDIR, /tmp, plus any writable_roots from the operator's Codex config) still apply. When the repository's git directory lives under one of those roots, the worker can write common `config`, `hooks/`, and the admin dir's `commondir`/`config.worktree`; the orchestrator's next git command there then runs worker-chosen config (core.fsmonitor, core.hooksPath, core.sshCommand) outside the sandbox. Harmed principal: the operator, via a prompt-injected or misbehaving Codex worker. The README's claim that 'placing a repository in a temp directory does not bypass the boundary' and that forged links 'get no grant' only holds for workers started at a validated linked-worktree root. Unverified against a live Codex (could not run it in this session): the evidence is the launcher code and the branch's own README rationale for the pins. The exposure exists at the base for all of these cases; the branch closes it for one case only.
builds/codex-git-admin/fix-2.md:22:suggestion: Emit the temp-directory exclusions (exclude_tmpdir_env_var, exclude_slash_tmp) for every Codex worker, or at least whenever `git rev-parse` finds a repository, outside the backlink check; keep only the --add-dir grants conditional on validation. Decide separately whether to clear writable_roots for all workers, since that also drops the pack-installed review-log and field-reports roots. Add a test asserting the pins are present for a normal checkout, a linked-worktree subdirectory and a forged-backlink worktree.
builds/codex-git-admin/fix-2.md:26:- f2: either grant what split-index needs (sharedindex.* files are created with new names, so the grant must allow that without granting commondir/gitdir/config.worktree), or detect core.splitIndex and say so plainly; say which and why.
packs/review-gate/skills/claude/security-check/SKILL.md:3:description: Standalone security check of the current branch's committed diff. A reviewer from the `security` role in ACS (Models → Roles) looks for security holes only, and every finding must quote the code it cites, checked by script. It reports findings and edits nothing. It is logged apart from the review gate, so it never closes a review, covers a push or counts as a PR's security review. Use when asked to "security check this", "look for security holes", or before trusting a risky change.
packs/review-gate/skills/claude/security-check/SKILL.md:9:Look at the committed diff for **security holes only**: trust boundaries crossed without a check, untrusted input reaching a shell, SQL, file paths, eval or HTML, authentication and authorization gaps, secrets in code or output, path traversal, unsafe deserialization, SSRF, and changes to dependencies, install scripts or CI. Correctness, style and performance are out of scope.
